# Top-rank pools (playtest 2, X2): at a club's or job's highest rank (`rank: 3`), its cards carry more risk and more
# reward instead of a quiet stat bump. They join the lower ranks' cards (pools.md); two per track.

# ---------------- Duelling Circle: Captain ----------------

@event duelling_captain_challenge
title: The Captain's Challenge
track: duelling
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: duel
tags: club duel
scene: A captain of a duelling circle in a chalk ring facing a visiting champion, the whole hall packed to the rafters.
text: {A visiting champion has challenged the Circle's captain, by name, in front of everyone. The captain is you.|Another challenger at the door, asking for the captain.|They come for the captain now, from three valleys away.} {~The hall is packed to the rafters.|{around(lyra): Lyra is in the front row with her arms folded.|The old captains are in the front row with their arms folded.}|Somebody is taking bets in the corridor.}
> left: Accept in front of everyone
  challenge combat expert drift mid:
    win:
      perform duelling +medium
      renown +medium
      health -small
      say: {~You win the third exchange and the hall comes apart. The champion bows, properly. The Circle will talk about it for a year.|A long, hard bout, and yours at the end. Your name goes on the board in gold paint.}
    lose:
      perform duelling -small
      renown -small
      health -small
      say: {~You lose, in front of everyone, by a breath. The Circle carries you to the infirmary anyway, which is its own verdict.|Beaten, cleanly. The champion is gracious. That's the worst part.}
> right: Send your best officer instead
  perform duelling +small
  mood -small
  say: {~Your officer wins, narrowly, and the hall cheers for the Circle. A few people look at you and wonder why you didn't step up.|You coach from the edge. The officer loses but fights well. It was the right call, you think, mostly.}

@event duelling_captain_injury
title: The Captain's Call
track: duelling
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: grim
repeat: 3
weight: 9
art: duel
tags: club duel danger
scene: A duellist kneeling in a chalk circle clutching a wrist, a captain crouching beside them, a referee waiting with a whistle.
text: {A first-year in the Circle has twisted a wrist in the final of the house cup, and insists on finishing.|Another final, another stubborn first-year with an injury.|The house cup final again, and again someone hurt who won't stop.} {~The house's honour is riding on it.|Their parents are in the gallery.|The referee looks at you: the captain decides.}
> left: Let them finish
  chance even:
    perform duelling +medium
    renown +small
    say: {~They finish, and win, with one hand. The gallery stands. You stood beside them, and you'll remember the fear more than the win.|They finish and win. Amaranth bandages them afterwards and gives you a look that could strip paint.}
  else:
    perform duelling -medium
    mood -small
    say: {~They finish and lose, and the wrist is worse. Sister Amaranth sends for you specifically, to explain.|The bout ends with a second injury. You let it happen. You don't sleep that night.}
> right: Stop the bout
  alignment +tiny
  perform duelling -small
  say: {~You call it. The house loses the cup and the first-year cries and calls you a coward. A week later, in the corridor, they thank you.|You stop it. The gallery boos. {around(lyra): Lyra, in the front row, nods once.|The referee, pocketing the whistle, nods once.}}

# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger: Editor ----------------

@event lumen_ledger_editor_scoop
title: The Story That Could Close Us
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: paper
tags: club
scene: A cramped newspaper office at night, a student editor reading a draft under a lamp, a stack of evidence beside it and the clock showing late.
text: {A reporter brings you a story about a governor of the academy, apparently true and well sourced, and dangerous.|Another story with teeth, on your desk with a dawn deadline.|Nessa calls it "the kind of story that closes papers". She says it admiringly.} {~The evidence is a stack of letters.|The source wants to stay unnamed.|The printer needs the pages by dawn.}
> left: Run it on the front page
  challenge research expert:
    win:
      perform lumen_ledger +large
      renown +medium
      alignment +tiny
      say: {~Every fact holds. The Ledger sells out by breakfast; the governor resigns by supper. Nessa frames the front page.|It runs, it holds, and the castle talks of nothing else for a week.}
    lose:
      perform lumen_ledger -medium
      renown -small
      gain detention
      say: {~One fact was wrong, and it's the one they sue over. The Ledger prints a retraction; you write it. Detention, too, for good measure.|It runs, with a mistake in it. The governor's lawyers are very polite, and very thorough.}
> right: Hold it until it's airtight
  perform lumen_ledger +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {~You hold it. Another paper in the village runs a thinner version first. Nessa doesn't speak to you for two days, then admits you were right.|You hold it for a month and check every line. When it finally runs, it's bulletproof, and old news.}

@event lumen_ledger_editor_threat
title: A Letter to the Editor
track: lumen_ledger
rank: 3
class: incident
tone: grim
repeat: 3
weight: 8
art: letter
tags: club danger
scene: A black-edged letter open on a newspaper desk, a student editor reading it with a cold face, the window behind them dark.
text: {A letter to the editor, unsigned, about a story you ran. It knows where you sleep.|Another unsigned letter, the same careful hand.|The letters have stopped being polite.} {~It smells of expensive ink.|The seal has been scraped off.|It's been slipped under the office door, not posted.}
> left: Print it, with a reply
  renown +small
  perform lumen_ledger +small
  mood -medium
  say: {~You print it in full on page two, with three lines of your own beneath. The castle loves it. You check your door twice every night for a month.|The letter goes in the paper. The next one takes a month to come. The waiting for it is worse.}
> right: Take it to the headmistress
  meet isolde
  rel isolde +small
  perform lumen_ledger -tiny
  say: {~She reads it once and puts it in a drawer. "Leave it with me." For a long while, you don't get another. Nessa says that's censorship. It's also sleep.|The headmistress handles it. You don't ask how. The Ledger is quieter for a while.}

# ---------------- Verdant Circle: Head Gardener ----------------

@event verdant_head_gardener_blight
title: The Blight
track: verdant
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: grim
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: leaf
tags: club nature
scene: A greenhouse full of wilting plants, black spots on every leaf, a head gardener holding a sickly seedling up to the light.
text: {Blight in the greenhouses, black spots spreading by the hour. The Head Gardener decides what to burn.|The blight is back, in a different house.|Black spots again. You know the smell of it now.} {~{around(tobias): Tobias is close to tears.|The Circle's youngest member is close to tears.}|The rarest plant in the collection is at the edge of it.|Maud has come up from the cottage to look.}
> left: Try an untested cure on everything
  challenge brew expert unlikely:
    win:
      perform verdant +large
      alchemy +small
      say: {~The cure works, overnight. Every plant lives, even the rare one. Maud says she's never seen it done, and asks for the recipe.|By morning the spots are fading. The Circle sings, badly, in the greenhouse.}
    lose:
      perform verdant -medium
      mood -medium
      say: {~The cure kills what the blight hadn't. You burn the whole house. {around(tobias): Tobias helps|The Circle helps}, and nobody blames you, which is worse.|It fails. You lose half the collection, and all of your confidence for a week.}
> right: Burn the sick plants now
  perform verdant +small
  mood -small
  say: {~You burn a third of the house and save the rest. It's the right call and it smells of grief.|You burn the sick ones before it spreads. The rare plant is among them.}

@event verdant_head_gardener_show
title: The Valley Fair
track: verdant
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 3
weight: 9
art: flower
tags: club nature
scene: A crowded show tent in a village field, prize vegetables on trestle tables, a student head gardener carrying an enormous potted plant toward the judges.
text: {The valley's flower show, and the Verdant Circle's entry is yours to choose.|The flower show again; the judges remember you.|The show tent smells of crushed grass and rivalry.} {~A farmer's pumpkin is the size of a cart.|Thornwick has sent a team.|The judges are three old women who have seen everything.}
> left: Enter the night-blooming lily
  challenge brew adept- unlikely wild:
    win:
      perform verdant +medium
      renown +small
      gold +small
      say: {~It opens at dusk in front of the judges, as if on cue. First prize, and a purse, and a drawing in the village paper.|The lily wins, narrowly, over a cabbage. Nobody will forget the cabbage's grower's face.}
    lose:
      perform verdant -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {~It refuses to open. The cabbage wins. The lily blooms at midnight, for nobody, in your arms.|Second place, to Thornwick. {around(tobias): Tobias says|The Circle says} second is good. It isn't.}
> right: Enter the reliable roses
  perform verdant +small
  say: {~Third prize for the roses. Solid, respectable, forgettable.|The roses place. The Circle is pleased; the Circle is also bored.}

# ---------------- Library Assistant: Senior Clerk ----------------

@event library_senior_banned_book
title: A Request for a Banned Book
track: library_assistant
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: book
tags: job study
scene: A library desk at closing time, a nervous first-year holding a request slip for a chained book, a senior clerk with the keys looking down at it.
text: {A first-year brings you a request slip for a chained book. Senior clerks hold the keys, and the rules say no.|Another request for a chained book, from a student who looks desperate.|They've heard you hold the keys. The slips keep coming.} {~The first-year's essay depends on it.|The slip is signed by a professor, perhaps.|The book in question bites.}
> left: Fetch it, against the rules
  challenge deceive adept-:
    win:
      wage
      gold +small
      alignment -tiny
      say: {~You fetch it, watch them read it, and chain it back before closing. Nobody knows. The first-year passes with honours and leaves you a cake with crowns folded under it.|The book goes out and comes back, with a grateful envelope. The rules bent, and didn't break.}
    lose:
      wage short
      perform library_assistant -medium
      say: {~The head librarian finds the chain unlocked. Half your pay docked and a long, quiet talk.|Caught, with the key in your hand, and docked for it. You're still Senior Clerk; nobody quite knows how.}
> right: Refuse
  wage
  perform library_assistant +small
  mood -small
  say: {~You say no. The first-year leaves in tears. The head librarian, who heard, nods.|The rules hold. So does your conscience, more or less.}

@event library_senior_night_flood
title: Water in the Stacks
track: library_assistant
rank: 3
class: incident
tone: grim
repeat: 2
weight: 8
art: wave
tags: job danger
scene: A flooded lower library level at night, water lapping at the bottom shelves, a senior clerk wading with an armful of books.
text: {A pipe bursts in the lower stacks at the end of your shift, and the senior clerk on duty is you.|Water in the stacks again. The pipes are older than the books.|The lower level floods every spring now.} {~The rarest books are on the bottom shelf.|The water is rising.|Someone is shouting for help two floors up.}
> left: Save the rare books first
  wage overtime
  perform library_assistant +medium
  health -medium
  say: {~You carry armful after armful up the stair in freezing water until long after closing. Every rare book saved. Your boots are not.|You save the rarest and lose the common ones, and the head librarian, arriving at last, hugs you, which nobody has ever seen.}
> right: Run for the head librarian
  wage
  perform library_assistant -tiny
  say: {~By the time she comes, a shelf is lost. "You did right to fetch me," she says, and looks at the shelf.|She takes charge. You carry what she points at.}

# ---------------- The Gilded Newt: Manager ----------------

@event tavern_manager_inspector
title: The Inspector Calls
track: tavern
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: job money
scene: A village tavern in the morning, a stern inspector in a long coat running a finger along a bar, a student manager following with a ledger.
text: {The licensing inspector is here, unannounced, and the landlady is away. The manager is you.|The inspector again, with the same long coat and longer list.|The inspector nods at you like an old enemy.} {~The cellar has a leak.|The ledger has a page you'd rather they didn't read.|There's a goblin asleep in the back booth.}
> left: Show everything, honestly
  wage
  challenge negotiate adept:
    win:
      perform tavern +large
      gold +small
      say: {~The inspector finds the leak, notes it and passes the Newt with a warning. The landlady, back, gives you a bonus and her late mother's tankard.|A clean bill, almost. The inspector shakes your hand.}
    lose:
      perform tavern -medium
      gold -small
      say: {~The fine is steep, the landlady takes a share out of your wages, and her face is steeper.|Three infractions. The till covers most of the fine. Your own wages cover the rest.}
> right: Steer them away from the cellar
  wage
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive adept:
    win:
      perform tavern +medium
      say: {~The inspector never goes downstairs. You fix the leak that night with your own hands, guiltily.|A clean bill, by omission. You don't tell the landlady how.}
    lose:
      perform tavern -medium
      gain watched
      say: {~The inspector goes straight to the cellar. The fine doubles for "obstruction". Bram hears of it somehow.|Caught steering. The inspector writes your name down in a separate book.}

@event tavern_manager_grisk_offer
title: An Investor
track: tavern
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 2
weight: 8
when: met(grisk) and not flag(grisk_gone)
art: pouch
tags: job money danger
scene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a tavern's best table, a bag of coin and a contract in front of him, a student manager standing opposite.
text: {Grisk would like to invest in the Newt, with new chairs and a second fire, "and a little room upstairs for my associates".|Grisk again, with a bigger bag of coin.|"My offer stands," says Grisk, "and grows."} {~The chairs really are falling apart.|The landlady is away.|The bag of coin is very heavy.}
> left: Take his money for the chairs
  wage
  gold +medium
  alignment -small
  perform tavern +small
  flag grisk_contact
  say: {~New chairs, a second fire, a fat "manager's commission" in your pocket, and Grisk's associates upstairs on Thursdays. Trade is up. So is the noise, at midnight.|Grisk pays you a finder's cut in a sealed envelope. The Newt has never looked better. The upstairs room has never been busier, and you don't go up there.}
> right: Turn him down
  wage
  perform tavern -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {~"Your loss," says Grisk, and means it as a threat. The chairs stay wobbly.|You say no. Grisk tips his hat and drinks somewhere else for a month, which hurts trade.}

# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk: Assistant Apothecary ----------------

@event apothecary_assistant_poison
title: A Dangerous Prescription
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: grim
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: bottle
tags: job alchemy danger
scene: An apothecary counter, a prescription slip with a dose underlined in red, a young assistant alone behind the counter frowning at it.
text: {A prescription comes in from a village healer with a dose ten times too strong. Pell is out. Assistant apothecaries decide.|Another slip with a strange dose. You've learned to read the healer's handwriting, which doesn't help.|A dose that would fell a horse, for an old shepherd.} {~The patient's grown son is waiting.|The healer is a day's ride away.|It might be a slip of the pen. It might not.}
> left: Mix it at a safe dose
  wage
  challenge brew adept:
    win:
      perform apothecary_clerk +large
      alchemy +small
      say: {~You work out what the healer meant and mix that. The patient recovers. Pell, back, reads the slip and goes white, then pats your shoulder for a long time.|Right dose, right remedy. The healer writes later to thank you, embarrassed.}
    lose:
      perform apothecary_clerk -medium
      mood -medium
      say: {~Your safe dose is too weak. The patient recovers slowly, and the son tells the whole village.|You get it nearly right. Nearly is a word you stop using for a while.}
> right: Refuse until the healer confirms
  wage short
  perform apothecary_clerk +tiny
  mood -small
  say: {~A day's wait. The patient's son shouts at you. The healer confirms the slip was wrong. You were right, and it was a long day.|You send a rider. The answer comes at midnight. You stay up for it.}

@event apothecary_assistant_rival_shop
title: A Shop Across the Square
track: apothecary_clerk
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 2
weight: 8
actor: pell
art: bottle
tags: job money
scene: A gleaming new apothecary shop across a village square, its window full of bright bottles, an old apothecary peering at it from his own doorway.
text: {A new apothecary has opened across the square, all brass and bright bottles and half Pell's prices.|The shop across the square has a queue now.|Pell has started calling the new shop "that brass insult".} {~Their remedies are mostly coloured water.|They've hired a singer for the doorway.|Pell's daughters have written to ask what's wrong.}
> left: Expose their coloured water
  wage
  challenge research adept-:
    win:
      perform apothecary_clerk +medium
      renown +small
      say: {~You buy a bottle of everything and test them in Pell's back room. The results go on a board in the window. The brass shop closes by the season's end.|Your tests are fair and public. The village comes back to Pell, a little sheepish.}
    lose:
      perform apothecary_clerk -small
      mood -small
      say: {~Their remedies turn out to work, annoyingly. You look petty. Pell says, "Well, now we know."|Inconclusive, and everyone saw you try.}
> right: Lower Pell's prices
  wage short
  perform apothecary_clerk +small
  say: {~Cheaper remedies, longer hours, a full shop. Pell grumbles and is secretly delighted.|You match them on price and beat them on care. It's slow, and it works.}

# ---------------- Broom Courier: Chief Courier ----------------

@event courier_chief_urgent_crossing
title: The Urgent Crossing
track: broom_courier
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 3
weight: 10
art: feather
tags: job danger
scene: A courier on a broom flying through a mountain pass in a gale, a red-sealed package clutched to their chest, lightning on the peaks.
text: {A red-sealed package for the far side of the mountains, by tomorrow, and only the chief courier flies red seals.|Another red seal, another gale over the pass.|The red seals come to you by name now.} {~The pass is in cloud.|A storm is coming in from the west.|The package is humming.}
> left: Fly the pass tonight
  wage overtime
  challenge feat expert wild:
    win:
      perform broom_courier +large
      renown +small
      health -small
      say: {~Through the pass in the dark, lightning on both sides. You deliver at dawn to a pale woman who breaks the seal and weeps with relief. The bonus is the biggest of your life.|You make it, frozen, at dawn. The recipient signs with shaking hands.}
    lose:
      perform broom_courier -medium
      health -large
      say: {~The gale takes you into a snowfield. You walk the last mile with the package and a broken wrist, and arrive late.|You crash short of the pass and are found by a goatherd. The package arrives a day late, via goat.}
> right: Wait for the storm to pass
  wage
  perform broom_courier -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {~You wait out the storm and fly at dawn. A day late. The recipient is gracious, and the loft-master isn't.|Late but whole. You'll always wonder what was in it.}

@event courier_chief_new_runner
title: The New Runner
track: broom_courier
rank: 3
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 3
weight: 9
art: feather
tags: job
scene: A courier loft at dawn, a nervous young runner holding a broom the wrong way round, a chief courier showing them the grip.
text: {The loft has a new runner, younger than you were, who holds the broom like a mop.|Another new runner, another bad grip.|The new runners all ask for you now.} {~Their first route is across the lake.|They've never flown in rain.|Their parents are watching from the gate.}
> left: Fly their first route with them
  wage short
  perform broom_courier +small
  mood +tiny
  say: {~You fly beside them across the lake, calling the wind. They land shaking and grinning. They'll name a broom after you, they say.|A slow route, flown twice. They'll be good, one day.}
> right: Let them learn on their own
  wage
  perform broom_courier +tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {~They come back soaked and late, with every parcel. You were the same.|They learn the hard way. So did you.}
